WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.713
Secure IPC needs validation workflow beyond the happy path.

2
00:00:03.713 --> 00:00:10.727
Logging should capture safe operational signals: connection events, peer category, message type, validation failure category, authorization denial,

3
00:00:10.727 --> 00:00:13.615
timeout, resource-limit rejection, disconnect, and restart.

4
00:00:13.615 --> 00:00:21.453
Logs should avoid secrets, sensitive message bodies, tokens, personal data, and raw data that support teams do not need.

5
00:00:21.453 --> 00:00:23.929
Monitoring should make local communication visible.

6
00:00:23.929 --> 00:00:32.180
Queue growth, repeated invalid messages, unexpected clients, stuck workers, crash loops, long request duration, and resource-limit rejections can all

7
00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.656
indicate compatibility problems or operational pressure.

8
00:00:34.656 --> 00:00:42.082
The goal is not to collect every byte; it is to know when the IPC boundary is unhealthy.

9
00:00:42.082 --> 00:00:49.095
Testing should include permissions, malformed messages, unsupported versions, boundary sizes, partial reads, out-of-order messages, timeouts,

10
00:00:49.095 --> 00:00:50.746
cancellation, shutdown, and compatibility.

11
00:00:50.746 --> 00:00:59.410
Parser-like IPC boundaries benefit from defensive fuzzing, sanitizers, static analysis, compiler warnings, and CI checks where they fit the codebase.

12
00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:03.948
Each fixed parser or lifecycle bug should gain a regression test.

13
00:01:03.948 --> 00:01:06.011
Reviews should ask concrete questions.

14
00:01:06.011 --> 00:01:07.248
Who can connect?

15
00:01:07.248 --> 00:01:08.899
How is identity verified?

16
00:01:08.899 --> 00:01:10.549
Which operations are authorized?

17
00:01:10.549 --> 00:01:12.199
Where are messages validated?

18
00:01:12.199 --> 00:01:13.850
What sizes are bounded?

19
00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:15.500
Can handles be transferred?

20
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.150
How are locks used?

21
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:19.625
What happens when a peer disappears?

22
00:01:19.625 --> 00:01:24.989
The best IPC reviews connect endpoint policy, code structure, tests, and operational signals.

23
00:01:24.989 --> 00:01:34.890
This workflow also supports incident response because teams can map a log category back to a contract rule, a permission decision, or a resource

24
00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:36.541
limit instead of guessing.

25
00:01:36.541 --> 00:01:45.617
Over time, those records clearly show whether the IPC boundary is stable or repeatedly failing in the same category across releases, upgrades,

26
00:01:45.617 --> 00:01:47.680
service changes, and platform changes.
